{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Point of Safe Return.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Point of Safe Return.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"point of safe return point of safe return","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Point of Safe Return.md","fileName":"Point of Safe Return.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Point of Safe Return","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 21:32\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Wind Navigation and Flight Range]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Wind Navigation and Flight Range","flightscience.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Point of Safe Return.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Point of Safe Return.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"point of safe return point of safe return","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Point of Safe Return.md","fileName":"Point of Safe Return.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Point of Safe Return","heading":"Point of Safe Return","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The point of safe return is the farthest point an aircraft can reach outbound and still return to its departure point with the planned usable endurance. Groundspeeds differ on the outbound and inbound legs when wind is present, and reserve, contingency, alternate, and ground fuel are excluded before safe endurance is computed. Stronger wind reduces the reachable PSR distance even when it shortens one of the two legs.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Wind Navigation and Flight Range","flightscience.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Point of Safe Return.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Point of Safe Return.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"point of safe return point of safe return","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Point of Safe Return.md","fileName